## 13. Web Interface & UCI Engine

Web Interface (web_control.py)

A lightweight Flask application that provides a graphical interface accessible from a browser on the same device (or local network).

Endpoints

· / – main page with links to analysis, play, training, and benchmarks.
· /analyse – Enter a FEN; the engine returns the best move, evaluation score, and principal variation.
· /play – Interactive board where you can make moves, and the engine replies (or human vs. human).
· /train – Triggers training and shows real‑time progress in the browser.
· /benchmark – Runs benchmark.py and displays the report.

Usage
Start the server: python3 web_control.py
Open http://localhost:5000 in Termux’s browser (or on a PC if port‑forwarded).

Security
Not hardened for public exposure. Use only on trusted networks.

UCI Engine (uci_engine.py)

Implements the Universal Chess Interface so the engine can be loaded by standard chess GUIs (Arena, CuteChess, BanksiaGUI, etc.) or used with Lichess bots.

Communication
Reads commands from stdin, writes responses to stdout.

Supported commands:

· uci – identify engine and options.
· isready – respond readyok.
· position [fen | startpos] moves ... – set up board.
· go [wtime] [btime] [movetime] [depth] ... – start search.
· stop – stop search and return bestmove.
· quit – exit.

Integration
To use with a GUI, add the engine with command: python3 /path/to/uci_engine.py.

---

## 14. Continuous Learning Loop

The engine is designed for self‑improvement through a closed loop:

```
1. Collect PGNs (self‑play, Lichess download, human games)
2. pgn_learner.py → new_dataset.pkl
3. Merge into master dataset
4. train.py → chess_nn_master.pkl
5. Activate (cp master -> chess_nn.pkl)
6. Play games (Engine vs Random, self‑play, or human)
## 7 Export those games as PGN → back to step 1
```

Automation
All steps can be automated with shell scripts. For example:

```bash
# auto_train.sh
python3 pgn_learner.py --input new_games.pgn --output new_data.pkl
python3 merge.py  # merges new_data into master
python3 train.py
cp chess_nn_master.pkl chess_nn.pkl
python3 web_control.py &
```

Frequency
On a phone, a full training cycle (10k samples, 3 epochs) takes only ~2 minutes. You can run it nightly, after accumulating a few hundred new positions.

Measuring improvement
Never assume the new model is better. After each cycle, run the evaluation suite (Section 15). Only keep the new model if it shows clear improvement in tactical tests or vs‑Random performance.

---

## 15. Evaluation & Benchmarking

Evaluation is a mandatory step after every training run. Do not skip it.

a) Regression Tests

```bash
bash run_tests.sh
```

Ensures the board, move generation, and feature cache are still bug‑free. Must pass 100%.

b) Perft

Perft (performance test) counts the number of legal move paths up to a given depth from a position. The script compares counts against known good values from standard chess positions.
Example: from startpos, depth 5 should be 4,865,609 nodes.

c) Tactical Suite

Create a list of FENs containing a hanging piece or a clear winning capture.
Use a simple script to query the engine at a fixed depth (e.g., depth 3) and check whether the best move is the expected capture. Compare the old model vs. the new model.

d) 100 Games vs Random

Run 100 games against a random mover (or a very weak fixed evaluator). Record win/draw/loss percentages.
Run with old model, then new model, and compare. Even a small increase (e.g., from 85% to 88% wins) indicates meaningful improvement.

e) Self‑Play

Play old model vs new model for, say, 50 games with symmetric conditions (same time per move). If the new model scores >50% (+ a margin), it’s genuinely stronger.

f) Benchmark Script (benchmark.py)

Reports:

· Nodes per second (NPS) from several test positions.
· Tactical accuracy (mate‑in‑1 solving).
· Self‑play stability (illegal moves, crashes).
  Output saved as benchmark_YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS.txt.

---

## 16. Performance & Memory

Training memory
For 10,940 samples (positions + values + policy indices):

· positions: ~10k ChessBoard objects → ~5 MB
· values and policies: negligible
· During training, the one‑hot matrix for a batch of 32 has shape (32, 20480) → ~2.5 MB
· Total RAM: < 50 MB, easily fits on a phone.

Training speed
On a typical Android phone (Snapdragon 8‑core), one epoch over 10k samples with batch size 32 takes ~30‑35 seconds. Three epochs finish in under 2 minutes.

Inference speed
A single forward pass (feature extraction + net evaluation) takes ~1‑2 ms. Within a search, many evaluations are cached via the transposition table, so the network is not the bottleneck.

Model size
chess_nn.pkl: ~1.5 MB (compressed pickle). Very portable.

Checkpoint size
Same as the model file. Keeping the last 5‑10 checkpoints uses negligible storage.

---

## 17. Troubleshooting

Problem Cause Solution
ERROR: training_data_5k_v2.pkl not a dict Old format (list/tuple) Convert using provided script (Section 8.2).
Training RAM explosion Model loaded incorrectly Ensure policy_is_indices=True in train() and that the data is integer indices, not one‑hot.
Engine plays illegal moves board.py bug or search returning invalid index Run bash run_tests.sh; fix move generation.
Model not improving Too few epochs, low learning rate Increase epochs or LR; add more training data.
Very slow search Transposition table too small or no move ordering Increase TT size, ensure policy head is being used for ordering.
Web interface not accessible Flask not installed pip install flask
UCI engine not responding I/O buffering issue Use python3 -u uci_engine.py to flush output.

---

## 18. FAQ

Q: Why 20480 moves?
Our encoding covers all possible from‑to squares (4032 basic moves) plus all promotion combinations, giving exactly 20480 indices. Many are illegal in any given position, but the policy head simply outputs a probability for each; illegal ones are masked during search.

Q: Why use pickle for datasets and models?
It natively serializes Python objects (like ChessBoard) without requiring custom converters. It’s fast and sufficient for a single‑user project. For production, one might switch to a more portable format (e.g., JSON with FEN strings), but pickle is convenient.

Q: Why not TensorFlow / PyTorch?
They are heavy dependencies that don’t run well on older Android devices. Our pure‑NumPy implementation gives full control and keeps the project dependencies minimal (only numpy and flask for the web UI).

Q: How do I resume training from a checkpoint?
Copy the desired checkpoint to chess_nn_master.pkl and run train.py again. It will continue from that state. However, the dataset will be trained again from scratch; we do not store optimizer state.

Q: Can I train on a PC and copy the model to my phone?
Yes. The .pkl files are architecture‑independent. Train on any machine, then copy chess_nn_master.pkl to the phone and rename it to chess_nn.pkl. The engine will use it immediately.

Q: What’s the ideal dataset size?
Even 10k samples improve the engine noticeably. For stronger play, aim for 100k‑500k positions. PGNs from master‑level games (Lichess Elite database) are excellent.

Q: How can I add my own features to the NN?
Modify extract_features() in nn.py. Add new scalars to the feature vector, increase INPUT_SIZE, and adjust the first layer weight shape. Retrain from scratch.

Q: The engine is weaker after training. Why?
Possible reasons: overfitting (use fewer epochs, add regularization), biased data (e.g., only losing games), or bugs in data generation. Always evaluate against the previous model.

---

## 19. Developer Guide

Modifying the Neural Network

· Change hidden layer sizes in DualNet.__init__().
· Add new features in extract_features(); update INPUT_SIZE.
· The training code in train() is self‑contained and can be replaced with a better optimizer (e.g., Adam).

Adding Search Enhancements

· All search logic is in search.py. To add aspiration windows, modify iterative_deepening().
· To experiment with different pruning, adjust null_move_pruning() or add futility pruning.

Dataset Generation

· pgn_learner.py can be extended to filter games by Elo, date, or opening.
· To generate capture data, write a script that scans positions for hanging pieces using the board’s attack maps.

Testing

· Run bash run_tests.sh after every code change.
· Add new perft positions to verify move generation correctness.

Coding Style

· Follow PEP 8 as much as possible.
· Keep functions small and self‑explanatory.
· Document any new features in this README.

